1
00:00:00.040 --> 00:00:01.158
Lesson three point two.

2
00:00:01.450 --> 00:00:03.394
How we tell the drum model how wrong it

3
00:00:03.394 --> 00:00:03.588
was.

4
00:00:07.470 --> 00:00:09.712
In lesson three point one the network

5
00:00:09.712 --> 00:00:12.309
ended in nine raw numbers for every slot,

6
00:00:12.309 --> 00:00:14.079
one per drum: the logits.

7
00:00:14.433 --> 00:00:16.026
Here are nine made up ones.

8
00:00:16.493 --> 00:00:18.656
Training compares them with what the real

9
00:00:18.656 --> 00:00:20.098
drummer played in that slot.

10
00:00:20.407 --> 00:00:22.312
And boils the comparison down to one

11
00:00:22.312 --> 00:00:24.836
number, the loss: how wrong the model was.

12
00:00:25.145 --> 00:00:27.514
Zero would be perfect; smaller is better.

13
00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:29.916
Training nudges every weight so this

14
00:00:29.916 --> 00:00:32.455
number goes down; lesson three point three

15
00:00:32.455 --> 00:00:32.983
shows how.

16
00:00:33.301 --> 00:00:34.993
Here we build the number itself.

17
00:00:38.353 --> 00:00:39.803
A logit can be any number.

18
00:00:40.138 --> 00:00:42.424
To compare it with a hit or a silence we

19
00:00:42.424 --> 00:00:45.046
want a probability: a number from zero to

20
00:00:45.046 --> 00:00:45.269
one.

21
00:00:45.807 --> 00:00:47.607
The sigmoid does it in three moves.

22
00:00:47.916 --> 00:00:49.253
Start from z, the logit.

23
00:00:49.561 --> 00:00:50.693
Take e to the minus z.

24
00:00:51.001 --> 00:00:53.161
e is two point seven one eight, and any

25
00:00:53.161 --> 00:00:54.498
power of it is above zero.

26
00:00:54.807 --> 00:00:56.504
Add one: now it's above one.

27
00:00:56.812 --> 00:00:57.995
And take one over that.

28
00:00:58.303 --> 00:01:00.309
One over something above one is always

29
00:01:00.309 --> 00:01:01.389
between zero and one.

30
00:01:02.250 --> 00:01:03.315
Three worked numbers.

31
00:01:03.620 --> 00:01:06.106
z zero: e to the zero is one, plus one is

32
00:01:06.106 --> 00:01:07.780
two, one over two: a half.

33
00:01:08.084 --> 00:01:09.809
A logit of zero means fifty fifty.

34
00:01:10.114 --> 00:01:12.397
z two: e to the minus two is zero point

35
00:01:12.397 --> 00:01:14.578
one three five, so p is zero point eight

36
00:01:14.578 --> 00:01:14.883
eight.

37
00:01:15.187 --> 00:01:17.470
z minus three: e to the three is twenty

38
00:01:17.470 --> 00:01:19.601
point one, so p is zero point zero four

39
00:01:19.601 --> 00:01:19.906
seven.

40
00:01:20.392 --> 00:01:22.429
Drawn over every logit, it's an S,

41
00:01:22.429 --> 00:01:24.518
flattening towards one on the right and

42
00:01:24.518 --> 00:01:26.659
zero on the left, never quite reaching

43
00:01:26.659 --> 00:01:27.025
either.

44
00:01:27.338 --> 00:01:28.749
Our three points sit on it.

45
00:01:32.107 --> 00:01:33.369
Why nine separate sigmoids?

46
00:01:33.650 --> 00:01:35.614
The usual alternative for picking between

47
00:01:35.614 --> 00:01:37.719
options is a softmax: it makes the nine

48
00:01:37.719 --> 00:01:39.590
numbers share a total of one, so they

49
00:01:39.590 --> 00:01:41.367
compete, and one drum wins the slot.

50
00:01:41.647 --> 00:01:42.910
But drums don't take turns.

51
00:01:43.191 --> 00:01:45.155
A kick and a closed hat often land on the

52
00:01:45.155 --> 00:01:45.857
same sixteenth.

53
00:01:46.137 --> 00:01:48.242
With nine sigmoids, each drum gets its own

54
00:01:48.242 --> 00:01:50.206
yes or no, and both can be sure at once.

55
00:01:51.081 --> 00:01:51.569
How often?

56
00:01:51.862 --> 00:01:53.717
Measured on a hundred and sixty-eight

57
00:01:53.717 --> 00:01:56.010
songs in September: of the slots with any

58
00:01:56.010 --> 00:01:58.255
drum in them, half hold one drum, and the

59
00:01:58.255 --> 00:01:59.622
other half hold two or more.

60
00:01:59.915 --> 00:02:01.720
Recomputed for this lesson on twelve

61
00:02:01.720 --> 00:02:03.819
training songs: almost half the kicks

62
00:02:03.819 --> 00:02:05.527
share their slot with a closed hat.

63
00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:07.820
So: nine sigmoids, nine yes or no

64
00:02:07.820 --> 00:02:08.309
questions.

65
00:02:11.607 --> 00:02:13.672
Now the loss for one drum in one slot.

66
00:02:13.998 --> 00:02:16.335
The idea: look at the probability the

67
00:02:16.335 --> 00:02:18.237
model gave to what really happened.

68
00:02:18.563 --> 00:02:20.031
High is good, low is bad.

69
00:02:20.523 --> 00:02:22.896
First case: the drum hit, so y is one.

70
00:02:23.213 --> 00:02:25.270
The cost is minus the natural log of p.

71
00:02:25.587 --> 00:02:27.750
If the model said zero point nine, the

72
00:02:27.750 --> 00:02:29.385
cost is zero point one oh five.

73
00:02:29.701 --> 00:02:31.864
If it said a half, zero point six nine

74
00:02:31.864 --> 00:02:32.181
three.

75
00:02:32.497 --> 00:02:34.871
If it said zero point zero one, four point

76
00:02:34.871 --> 00:02:35.293
six one.

77
00:02:35.610 --> 00:02:37.772
Drawn, the cost shoots up as p goes to

78
00:02:37.772 --> 00:02:38.036
zero.

79
00:02:38.498 --> 00:02:40.599
Second case: no hit, y is zero.

80
00:02:40.930 --> 00:02:43.141
Now the probability of what happened is

81
00:02:43.141 --> 00:02:45.574
one minus p, so the cost is minus the log

82
00:02:45.574 --> 00:02:46.403
of one minus p.

83
00:02:46.734 --> 00:02:49.111
Saying zero point one costs zero point one

84
00:02:49.111 --> 00:02:49.554
oh five.

85
00:02:49.885 --> 00:02:52.373
Saying zero point nine nine, sure of a hit

86
00:02:52.373 --> 00:02:54.805
that never came, costs four point six one.

87
00:02:55.137 --> 00:02:56.076
The mirror image.

88
00:02:56.503 --> 00:02:58.994
Compare confident and right, zero point

89
00:02:58.994 --> 00:03:01.544
one oh five, with confident and wrong,

90
00:03:01.544 --> 00:03:02.671
four point six one.

91
00:03:03.027 --> 00:03:04.510
Forty-four times as much.

92
00:03:04.866 --> 00:03:07.297
The log punishes a confident mistake far

93
00:03:07.297 --> 00:03:09.373
harder than it rewards a confident

94
00:03:09.373 --> 00:03:09.847
success.

95
00:03:10.806 --> 00:03:13.335
Both cases fit in one line, the binary

96
00:03:13.335 --> 00:03:14.198
cross-entropy.

97
00:03:14.568 --> 00:03:16.789
When y is one, the second term is

98
00:03:16.789 --> 00:03:19.441
multiplied by zero and vanishes: only

99
00:03:19.441 --> 00:03:20.675
minus log p is left.

100
00:03:21.045 --> 00:03:24.006
When y is zero, the first term vanishes:

101
00:03:24.006 --> 00:03:25.856
only minus log of one minus p.

102
00:03:27.039 --> 00:03:29.499
In our code the loss is PyTorch's binary

103
00:03:29.499 --> 00:03:31.059
